Verified Multi-Agent Intelligence (VMAI)

A Formal Mathematical Architecture for Reliable Autonomous AI Systems

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Claude, chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta 

Abstract

Autonomous multi‑agent systems constructed from large language models exhibit hallucination‑driven errors, cascading plan failures, and an absence of verifiable guarantees. This dissertation presents Verified Multi‑Agent Intelligence (VMAI), an architecture in which every externally consequential action must carry a machine‑checkable certificate of correctness before execution. VMAI unifies four formally specified layers: (i) a confidence‑weighted, Byzantine‑robust consensus layer over heterogeneous agent outputs, (ii) a temporal‑logic formal verification layer operating on synthesised plans, (iii) a three‑valued runtime monitor enforcing safety at the action level, and (iv) a Bayesian reliability layer that updates agent trust from accumulated execution evidence. All layers are defined on a single filtered probability space, and their guarantees are expressed in a common stochastic framework.

The dissertation proves: an exponential tail bound on the probability of consensus error under calibrated, independent agents; soundness and abstraction‑refinement completeness of the plan verifier for the safety fragment of Linear Temporal Logic; soundness of the LTL₃ runtime monitor for safety properties, together with a formal impossibility result for the enforcement of arbitrary liveness by truncation monitors; almost‑sure convergence of Beta‑Bernoulli trust estimates under exchangeable evidence; and an end‑to‑end composition theorem that bounds the probability of unsafe execution using the Fréchet–Hoeffding inequality and an exact correlation identity, establishing graceful degradation without requiring statistical independence between layers. Two necessity theorems demonstrate that neither the consensus‑plus‑monitor combination nor an uncalibrated confidence mechanism can, by itself, achieve sub‑constant error bounds for tasks requiring liveness. A computational cost model and a falsifiable evaluation protocol are provided. The work closes with a discussion of the framework’s assumptions, limitations, and avenues for future extension.

Keywords: agentic AI, formal verification, runtime enforcement, LTL₃, Byzantine consensus, proof‑carrying decisions, multi‑agent systems.

