1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The transition from conversational language models to autonomous, tool‑using, multi‑step agents has exposed a structural gap between capability and trustworthiness. Empirical audits show that even frontier models achieve only approximately 41% step‑localisation accuracy on agent hallucination benchmarks, with tool‑use hallucinations the most difficult category to detect [1]. Failures in memory and reflection modules cascade through subsequent decisions, causing complete task failure [2]. Industry has responded with a maker–checker pattern: a Planner decomposes goals, specialist Executors act, and a Verifier checks the result. Yet decomposition alone does not yield correctness: planners are disproportionately vulnerable to prompt injection and memory poisoning, and LLM‑based self‑verification is known to be unreliable [3].

In parallel, the formal methods community has produced a series of point solutions: runtime enforcement with customisable rule languages (AgentSpec), probabilistic model checking via learned Markov chains (Pro2Guard), SMT‑guided constrained generation with backtracking (Agent‑C), and Byzantine‑style consensus protocols for LLM agents (CP‑WBFT) [4,5]. What has been missing is a unifying mathematical theory that shows how consensus, formal verification, runtime enforcement, and probabilistic trust compose into a single soundness guarantee. This dissertation provides that theory.

1.2 Central Hypothesis

H. For any multi‑agent system whose actions are gated by the conjunction of (a) a calibrated weighted‑consensus acceptance test, (b) a model‑checked plan certificate, and (c) a three‑valued runtime monitor, the probability that an unsafe action reaches the environment is bounded above by a computable function of the agents’ individual fault rates, the verifier’s model‑fidelity gap, and the correlation between consensus and model errors—and this bound degrades gracefully (non‑catastrophically) as any single layer weakens.

A formal version of this hypothesis is proved in §7 (Theorems 7.1 and 7.2).

1.3 Related Work

The dominant 2024–2026 line treats agent safety as a specification problem: translate natural‑language policy into a formal object (LTL, DSL rules, or first‑order constraints) and enforce it either offline before deployment or online at each decision point [6]. Runtime verification, rooted in decades of formal methods, specifies what an agent must and must not do in temporal logic, synthesises a monitor from that specification, and enforces the result at every decision point [7]. AgentSpec demonstrates that natural‑language policies can be partially auto‑formalised, achieving 95.56% precision and 70.96% recall for embodied agents [8]. The consensus‑over‑LLM‑agents literature reframes unreliable models as Byzantine nodes, with CP‑WBFT weighting each agent’s vote by a self‑reported confidence probe, tolerating fault rates far beyond the classical one‑third threshold [9]. Complementary work shows that voting protocols outperform free‑form debate on reasoning tasks by a wide margin [10]. The concept of proof‑carrying decisions—cryptographically‑signed objects encoding custody, lineage, and policy gates—provides the governance framework into which VMAI’s certificate object naturally fits [11]. Empirical failure taxonomies confirm that existing safety benchmarks under‑cover the precise failure modes VMAI targets: hallucination‑driven action and multi‑step error cascading remain weakly evaluated [12]. These findings collectively motivate both the architecture and the evaluation protocol proposed in this dissertation.

