2. Formal Preliminaries

We fix a filtered probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \ge 0}, \mathbb{P}), where \mathcal{F}_t represents the system’s observable history through decision epoch t. All random objects introduced below are \mathcal{F}_t-measurable unless stated otherwise. \mathbb{1}[\cdot] denotes the indicator function; \mathrm{Var}, \mathrm{Cov}, \mathrm{Corr} denote variance, covariance, and Pearson correlation. For any set X, X^* is the set of finite sequences over X.

Definition 2.1 (Task, Ground Truth). We consider decision tasks with a finite action space Y \subseteq \mathcal{A}. There exists a deterministic oracle \phi_{\text{true}} : Y \to \{0,1\} such that \phi_{\text{true}}(y)=1 iff y is correct. The correct action y_{\text{true}} \in Y is the unique element with \phi_{\text{true}}(y_{\text{true}})=1. If no such element exists, the task is ill‑posed and the system aborts.

Definition 2.2 (Agent). An agent A_i is characterised by:

· a latent adversarial status \iota_i \in \{0,1\} (possibly adaptively chosen);
· for non‑adversarial agents (\iota_i = 0), an intrinsic error probability e_i \in [0,1] such that, conditional on the history and \iota_i = 0, Z_i := \mathbb{1}[Y_i = y_{\text{true}}] \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(1-e_i);
· an action proposal Y_i : \Omega \to Y and a self‑reported confidence p_i \in [0,1], both \mathcal{F}_t-measurable.

Definition 2.3 (Calibration). Agent i is \gamma_i-calibrated if, almost surely,

\bigl| \mathbb{P}(Y_i = y_{\text{true}} \mid p_i) - p_i \bigr| \le \gamma_i

for some constant \gamma_i \in [0,1). The agent is perfectly calibrated when \gamma_i = 0.

Definition 2.4 (VMAI System). A VMAI instance is a tuple \mathcal{M} = (\{A_i\}_{i=1}^n, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{V}, \mathcal{R}) where:

· \{A_i\}_{i=1}^n is a finite population of agents (one designated Planner, k Specialists, plus auxiliary Verifier/Monitor agents);
· \mathcal{C} is the consensus operator (§3);
· \mathcal{V} is the formal plan verifier (§4);
· \mathcal{R} is the runtime monitor (§5).

The system maintains Bayesian trust weights w_i \ge 0, \sum_i w_i = 1, which are \mathcal{F}_{t-1}-measurable (i.e., fixed prior to the agents casting their votes at epoch t).

Definition 2.5 (Candidate Action, Certificate). At decision epoch t, each agent proposes a candidate action y_i \in Y with confidence p_i. A certificate for an action y is a tuple \kappa(y) = (V_{\text{cons}}(y), V_{\text{form}}(y), V_{\text{rt}}(y)) \in \{0,1\}^3. Execution is permitted iff \kappa(y) = (1,1,1).

