3. The Consensus Layer

3.1 Weighted Confidence Score

Given trust weights w_i and agent reports (Y_i, p_i), define the weighted confidence score for each candidate y \in Y:

C(y) = \sum_{i=1}^n w_i \, p_i \, \mathbb{1}[Y_i = y].

The accepted output is

y^* = \arg\max_{y \in Y} C(y), \qquad V_{\text{cons}}(y^*) = \mathbb{1}\bigl[ C(y^*) \ge \tau \bigr],

with acceptance threshold \tau \in (1/2, 1].

3.2 Assumptions

Assumption 3.1 (Conditional Independence). Given the history \mathcal{F}_{t-1}, the random pairs (Y_i, p_i) are mutually independent across agents.

Assumption 3.2 (Bounded Confidence Gap). There exist constants 0 < p_{\min}, p_{\max} < 1 such that almost surely,

· if Y_i = y_{\text{true}} (agent votes correctly), then p_i \ge p_{\min};
· if Y_i = y' \neq y_{\text{true}} and all incorrect agents coordinate on the same wrong value y', then p_i \le p_{\max}.

3.3 Exponential Error Bound

Let W_h = \sum_{i: Z_i = 1} w_i be the total weight of correct votes, and W_f = 1 - W_h the complementary weight. In the worst case, all incorrect votes concentrate on a single wrong value y'. Define \mu_h = \mathbb{E}[C(y_{\text{true}})].

Theorem 3.1 (Probabilistic Consensus Bound). Under Assumptions 3.1 and 3.2, for any \epsilon > 0,

\mathbb{P}\bigl( C(y_{\text{true}}) \le \mu_h - \epsilon \bigr) \le \exp\!\left( -\frac{2\epsilon^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2} \right). \tag{3.1}

Moreover, if there exists \delta > 0 such that for every incorrect candidate y',

\mu_h - \mathbb{E}[C(y')] \ge \delta, \tag{3.2}

then

\mathbb{P}( y^* \neq y_{\text{true}} ) \le 2 \exp\!\left( -\frac{\delta^2}{2\sum_i w_i^2} \right). \tag{3.3}

Proof. Under Assumption 3.1, C(y_{\text{true}}) = \sum_i w_i p_i \mathbb{1}[Y_i = y_{\text{true}}] is a sum of independent random variables bounded in [0, w_i]. Hoeffding’s inequality (Hoeffding, 1963) gives

\mathbb{P}\!\left( \sum_i X_i - \mathbb{E}\!\sum_i X_i \le -\epsilon \right) \le \exp\!\left( -\frac{2\epsilon^2}{\sum_i (b_i - a_i)^2} \right)

with b_i = w_i, a_i = 0, yielding (3.1).

For the second claim, the error event \{y^* \neq y_{\text{true}}\} implies there exists some incorrect y' with C(y_{\text{true}}) \le C(y'). By (3.2), \mathbb{E}[C(y_{\text{true}})] - \mathbb{E}[C(y')] \ge \delta. Apply (3.1) to C(y_{\text{true}}) with \epsilon = \delta/2, and a symmetric Hoeffding bound to C(y') exceeding its mean by \delta/2. A union bound over the two tail events gives the bound 2\exp(-\delta^2/(2\sum w_i^2)). ∎

Corollary 3.1 (Equal Weights, Classical Threshold). With equal weights w_i = 1/n and perfect calibration, condition (3.2) reduces to (1 - W_f) > W_f, i.e., W_f < 1/2. This is the crash‑fault honest‑majority threshold. The more stringent Byzantine bound of 1/3 does not apply here because each agent is bound by the certificate mechanism to a single signed vote per epoch; equivocation is structurally excluded.

3.4 Remark on Split Voting

If the adversarial weight is split across m distinct wrong values, the expected score of any single incorrect candidate is at most W_f p_{\max}/m, which is smaller than the concentrated case. Thus split voting only widens the margin condition (3.2), making the bound tighter. No additional lemma is required.

