4. The Formal Verification Layer

4.1 Specification Language

We restrict the specification language to the syntactic safety fragment of Linear Temporal Logic (LTL):

\Phi_{\text{safe}} = \{\, \Box\psi \mid \psi \text{ is a Boolean combination of atomic propositions and past‑LTL operators} \,\}.

This fragment contains all properties enforceable by runtime truncation monitors (§5).

Definition 4.1 (Execution Model). A plan \pi produced by the Planner induces a finite Kripke structure M_\pi = (S, s_0, \to, L) where S is a finite set of control locations, \to \subseteq S \times S is the transition relation, and L : S \to 2^{\mathrm{AP}} labels each state with the atomic propositions true at that program point.

Assumption 4.1 (Sound Labeling). The labeling L is sound with respect to the real environment: if \mathrm{unsafe} \in L(s), then every concrete execution that reaches the state corresponding to s violates the environment’s safety condition. The labeling may be incomplete (a missing unsafe proposition does not guarantee safety).

4.2 Plan Verification via Model Checking

For a specification \varphi = \Box\psi, we construct a Büchi automaton \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} that accepts precisely the traces violating \Box\psi. Because \psi is a safety property, \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} is a deterministic finite automaton recognising bad prefixes: finite words that contain a violation of \psi. Model checking reduces to reachability in the product M_\pi \otimes \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi}: an accepting state of the product is reachable iff some execution trace of M_\pi violates \varphi. Define

V_{\text{form}}(\varphi, \pi) = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if no accepting state is reachable in } M_\pi \otimes \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi},\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Theorem 4.1 (Soundness). If V_{\text{form}}(\varphi, \pi) = 1, then every concrete execution that respects the control flow of \pi and the sound labeling satisfies \varphi.

Proof. Any concrete violation of \Box\psi would produce a finite bad prefix accepted by \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi}. Because the labeling is sound, the abstract trace of this violating execution is a path in M_\pi whose state labels include the propositions witnessing the violation. That path induces an accepting run in the product. V_{\text{form}}=1 asserts that no such run exists, so no concrete violation can occur. ∎

Theorem 4.2 (Abstraction‑Refinement Completeness). If V_{\text{form}}(\varphi, \pi) = 0, the model checker returns a finite counterexample trace \sigma in M_\pi. This trace is either real (can be instantiated in a high‑fidelity simulator) or spurious (due to over‑approximation in L). There exists a computable refinement procedure that, for spurious counterexamples, adds a new atomic proposition to distinguish the abstract state from the actual concrete reachable set, eliminating that spurious path. The CEGAR loop terminates for finite S.

Proof. Standard counterexample‑guided abstraction refinement; the finite state space guarantees termination. ∎

Remark 4.1 (Complexity). For \psi purely propositional (no temporal operators), \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} has size O(|\psi|) and reachability in the product is checked in time O(|M_\pi| \cdot |\psi|). The general exponential O(2^{|\psi|}) bound for LTL model checking applies only when \psi itself contains nested temporal operators, which our fragment excludes. The cost model (§8) therefore uses the linear bound as the typical case, with the exponential figure retained as a conservative upper bound for future extensions.

