5. Runtime Verification and Bayesian Reliability

5.1 LTL₃ Runtime Monitor

We synthesize a runtime monitor for \varphi = \Box\psi following the LTL₃ construction of Bauer et al. (2011). Given a finite trace prefix \tau_{0:t}, the monitor returns a verdict in \{\top, \bot, ?\}:

· \bot (reject) if \tau_{0:t} is a bad prefix—every infinite continuation violates \varphi;
· \top (accept) if the prefix is a good prefix;
· ? (inconclusive) otherwise.

For \Box\psi, a bad prefix is any finite sequence that contains a state in which \psi is false. The monitor is implemented as a Moore automaton that emits \bot exactly when the next proposed action would transition the system into a state where \psi evaluates to false.

Theorem 5.1 (Monitor Soundness). For a correctly synthesized monitor enforcing \varphi = \Box\psi by blocking the action that would cause the next state to violate \psi, every finite executed trace satisfies \Box\psi.

Proof. By construction, the monitor raises \bot iff the next state would make \psi false. Since the system blocks that action, the executed trace ends before any violation occurs. The resulting finite trace contains no state where \psi is false; hence it satisfies \Box\psi under finite‑trace semantics. ∎

5.2 Liveness Impossibility

Lemma 5.2 (Limitation of Truncation Monitors). A truncation monitor—one whose only enforcement mechanism is to halt execution (Schneider 2000)—cannot enforce an arbitrary LTL liveness property \Diamond\,\text{goal} without either violating safety or causing indefinite blocking.

Proof sketch. If the environment (or an adversarial agent) can consistently avoid \text{goal} while never triggering \neg\text{unsafe}, the monitor has no action it can block that forces progress, because blocking only removes transitions, it never inserts new ones. The monitor can preserve safety by blocking forever, but that violates liveness. This is a direct consequence of the classic result that the set of properties enforceable by truncation monitors is exactly the class of safety properties (Ligatti, Bauer, and Walker 2005). ∎

Remark 5.1. The impossibility is specific to truncation monitors. Richer enforcement automata—edit automata—can enforce a larger class, including certain bounded‑liveness properties, by buffering and delaying actions. Extending the VMAI runtime layer to an edit automaton is future work.

5.3 Bayesian Trust Update

Each agent i is associated with an unknown error probability e_i \in [0,1]. We place a \text{Beta}(a, b) prior over 1-e_i (the probability of correctness). After observing K independent task outcomes, let S_i be the number of times agent i was correct. The likelihood for S_i given e_i is \text{Binomial}(K, 1-e_i). By Beta‑Binomial conjugacy, the posterior of 1-e_i given the data is \text{Beta}(a + S_i,\; b + K - S_i).

We define the trust weight as the posterior mean:

w_i = \mathbb{E}[1 - e_i \mid S_i] = \frac{a + S_i}{a + b + K}.

These weights are then normalised: \tilde{w}_i = w_i / \sum_{j=1}^n w_j.

Assumption 5.1 (Exchangeability). The sequence of decision tasks is exchangeable for each agent, so that the Bernoulli model with a constant e_i is well‑specified.

Proposition 5.3 (Consistency). Under the Beta‑Bernoulli model and exchangeability,

\tilde{w}_i \xrightarrow{a.s.} \frac{1 - e_i}{\sum_{j=1}^n (1 - e_j)} \quad \text{as } K \to \infty.

Consequently, if there exists at least one agent with error rate e_i < \epsilon and all other agents have error rates \ge \epsilon, the normalised weight of any high‑error agent converges to 0 almost surely.

Proof. By the Strong Law of Large Numbers, S_i/K \to 1 - e_i almost surely. The posterior mean (a+S_i)/(a+b+K) is a weighted average of the prior mean and the empirical frequency; as K \to \infty the prior’s influence vanishes, so w_i \to 1 - e_i a.s. Normalisation is continuous, so \tilde{w}_i converges to the normalised limit. The second claim follows by noting that if some agent has 1-e_i > 1-\epsilon and another has 1-e_j \le 1-\epsilon, the former’s weight tends to a positive constant while the latter’s tends to zero after normalisation. ∎

Remark 5.2 (Trust‑Consistency). Proposition 5.3 is the formal justification for the requirement that the Bayesian trust layer must accompany the consensus layer. Without it, uncalibrated or persistently faulty agents could retain undue influence indefinitely, as proven in Theorem 8.2.

