6. Unified Optimisation Objective

Define the system‑level objective over the joint policy space \Pi (a product of finite stochastic policy simplices, given finite state/action spaces and bounded plan depth):

J(\Pi) = \alpha A(\Pi) + \beta R(\Pi) + \gamma E(\Pi) - \delta C(\Pi),

where:

· A = task accuracy,
· R = reliability (probability all three gates pass),
· E = explainability (e.g., inverse certificate size),
· C = computational cost,
· \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0 are governance‑chosen weights.

Proposition 6.1 (Existence of an Optimal Configuration). If Y is finite, plans are drawn from a finite language, and the horizon T is bounded, then \Pi is a compact set in a finite‑dimensional Euclidean space. All four components A, R, E, C are continuous functions of the policy parameters. By the extreme value theorem, J attains a maximum on \Pi.

Proof. Under the stated finiteness assumptions, each agent’s policy is a stochastic matrix over a finite domain, i.e., a point in a product of simplices, which is compact. The objective components are continuous in these parameters (accuracy and reliability are expectation‑based; cost and explainability are deterministic functions of the policies). A continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum. ∎

Remark 6.1. Global optimality is intractable in practice, but the layered architecture decomposes the problem: trust weights are updated via convex Bayesian inference, consensus threshold is a scalar parameter, model checking is decidable offline, and runtime monitoring is linear‑time per step. This decomposition is the standard architectural justification for a layered rather than monolithic design.

