7. The Composition Theorem

We now prove the central result: the bound on the probability that an unsafe action is executed by the full VMAI stack.

Definition 7.1. Define the events:

· A = \{ V_{\text{cons}} = 1 \text{ but } y^* \neq y_{\text{true}} \} (consensus accepts an incorrect action),
· B = \{ V_{\text{form}} = 1 \text{ but the plan contains a reachable state that violates safety in the real environment} \}.

Let \epsilon_{\text{cons}} = \mathbb{P}(A), \epsilon_{\text{form}} = \mathbb{P}(B). By Theorem 5.1, the runtime monitor’s failure probability for safety properties is \epsilon_{\text{rt}} = 0, assuming correct monitor synthesis. Let \rho = \mathrm{Corr}(\mathbb{1}_A, \mathbb{1}_B) be the Pearson correlation between the two failure indicators.

Proposition 7.1 (Exact Correlation Identity). For any two events A, B with probabilities in (0,1),

\mathbb{P}(A \cap B) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}} + \rho \sqrt{ \epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}}) \, \epsilon_{\text{form}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{form}}) }. \tag{7.1}

Proof. By definition, \mathrm{Cov}(\mathbb{1}_A, \mathbb{1}_B) = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_A \mathbb{1}_B] - \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_A]\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_B] = \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) - \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}}. Also, \mathrm{Cov}(\mathbb{1}_A, \mathbb{1}_B) = \rho \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_A)\mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_B)}. For Bernoulli variables, \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_A) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}}), and similarly for B. Substituting and solving for \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) yields (7.1). ∎

Theorem 7.1 (Composition Bound).

(a) Fréchet–Hoeffding bound (always valid):

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe execution}) = \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) \le \min(\epsilon_{\text{cons}}, \epsilon_{\text{form}}). \tag{7.2}

(b) Correlation‑aware bound: Given an upper bound \hat{\rho} \ge \rho on the correlation (estimable from data),

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe execution}) \le \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}} + \hat{\rho} \sqrt{ \epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}}) \, \epsilon_{\text{form}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{form}}) }. \tag{7.3}

(c) Independence special case (\rho = 0): When consensus and formal verification failures arise from disjoint mechanisms,

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe execution}) \le \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}}. \tag{7.4}

Proof. (7.2) follows from A \cap B \subseteq A and A \cap B \subseteq B by monotonicity of \mathbb{P}. (7.3) follows from (7.1) and the monotonicity of the right‑hand side in \rho once \hat{\rho} \ge \rho is substituted. (7.4) is the special case \rho = 0. ∎

Corollary 7.1 (Graceful Degradation). All three bounds tend to 0 if either \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \to 0 or \epsilon_{\text{form}} \to 0. Thus no single layer is a single point of failure: degrading one layer while keeping the other strong still guarantees a safety improvement, satisfying the “graceful degradation” claim of Hypothesis H.

7.1 Total Undesired Execution Probability

We now bound the probability of any undesired execution—unsafe or merely incorrect.

Theorem 7.2 (Total Error Bound). Under the assumption that y_{\text{true}} is safe, \{\text{unsafe}\} \subseteq \{\text{incorrect}\} = I. Hence

\mathbb{P}(\text{undesired execution}) = \mathbb{P}(I) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}}.

The probability of an unsafe execution is at most \min(\epsilon_{\text{cons}}, \epsilon_{\text{form}}), while the remaining \epsilon_{\text{cons}} - \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) is the probability of executing an incorrect but safe action.

Proof. By Definition 7.1, I = \{y^* \neq y_{\text{true}}\} and A = \{V_{\text{cons}}=1 \text{ and } y^* \neq y_{\text{true}}\}. Because execution requires V_{\text{cons}}=1, the event \{\text{undesired execution}\} = I \cap \{V_{\text{cons}}=1\} = A, so its probability is \epsilon_{\text{cons}}. Since \{\text{unsafe execution}\} = A \cap B \subseteq A, the partition follows. ∎

Practical interpretation. Theorem 7.2 shows that the formal and runtime layers re‑partition the total error probability rather than reducing it. Reducing \epsilon_{\text{cons}} (by improving agent reliability, consensus weights, or calibration) is the only lever that decreases the total frequency of wrong actions. Formal verification is a safety multiplier, not a correctness multiplier. This fact is central to the architectural rationale: every layer has a distinct, non‑redundant role.

