8. Necessity Theorems and Cost Model

8.1 Necessity of the Formal Verifier

Theorem 8.1 (Liveness Requires Formal Verification). Consider any system that uses only consensus and runtime monitoring (no formal verifier). For any non‑safety LTL property (e.g., \Diamond\,\text{goal}), there exists an environment in which, regardless of the number of agents, the probability of failure (either safety violation or deadlock) is bounded away from zero. Hence a formal plan verifier is necessary to achieve high reliability for tasks with liveness requirements.

Proof sketch. A truncation monitor cannot force progress (Lemma 5.2). An adversary that consistently proposes actions that avoid both the goal and any safety violation will cause the system to loop safely forever, violating liveness. No consensus mechanism can distinguish this situation from genuine progress without a model of the plan’s intended liveness property, which is exactly what the formal verifier supplies. ∎

8.2 Necessity of Bayesian Trust

Theorem 8.2 (Calibration‑Free Consensus Vulnerability). If agents may report arbitrary confidence values without any calibration guarantee (\gamma_i may be arbitrarily large), then for any threshold \tau < 1, there exists an adversary controlling a minority of agents (W_f < 1/2) who can force the consensus to output an incorrect action with positive probability. Therefore, a mechanism that infers true reliability from longitudinal evidence (the Bayesian trust layer) is not optional.

Proof. The adversary sets p_i = 1 for a wrong value. If honest agents’ true confidences are lower than 1, the weighted sum for the incorrect value can exceed \tau despite the adversary’s minority weight. Without calibration or empirical trust estimates, the consensus cannot detect this bluff. ∎

8.3 Computational Cost Model

We quantify the per‑epoch overhead of the VMAI stack:

· Consensus: O(n) score evaluations.
· Model checking: For a propositional \psi, the automaton size is O(|\psi|) and emptiness checking is O(|M_\pi| \cdot |\psi|), i.e., linear in the plan size. The worst‑case bound for general LTL with nested temporal operators is PSPACE, but our safety fragment avoids this.
· Runtime monitor: O(1) per step (finite‑state machine lookup).
· Bayesian update: O(1) per agent (Beta parameter update).

Overall, C(\Pi) = O(n + |M_\pi| \cdot |\psi| + T), where T is the episode length. The model‑checking term dominates in practice but remains tractable for bounded plans.

