9. Evaluation Protocol

Because this dissertation is a theoretical contribution and contains no physical experiments or code training, the following protocol is proposed for future empirical validation.

9.1 Independent Layer Characterisation

1. Calibration measurement: Before deployment, estimate each agent’s calibration curves and determine p_{\min}, p_{\max} empirically on a held‑out set of tasks.
2. Consensus error rate \epsilon_{\text{cons}}: Run the consensus layer alone on a set of synthetic decision problems with known ground truth, varying the fault rates of participating agents. Measure the frequency of incorrect acceptance.
3. Formal‑verification miss rate \epsilon_{\text{form}}: Evaluate the plan verifier on a benchmark of plans with known safety violations (injection attacks, tool‑use errors). Count the proportion of unsafe plans that receive V_{\text{form}}=1.
4. Correlation estimation \hat{\rho}: From joint failure logs, compute the sample correlation between \mathbb{1}_A and \mathbb{1}_B. This quantity is required for the correlation‑aware bound (7.3) and, because (7.1) is an identity, is directly reportable.

9.2 End‑to‑End Safety

Run the full VMAI stack in adversarial environments (e.g., MCP tool‑injection scenarios, prompt‑poisoning attacks on the Planner) and measure the fraction of episodes in which an unsafe action is executed. Compare this empirical value against the theoretical bounds of Theorem 7.1.

9.3 Timeliness Metrics

Adopt Early Intervention Rate and Intervention Gap (as introduced by StepShield) to quantify how early the runtime monitor blocks unsafe actions relative to irreversible side effects. Theorem 5.1 guarantees eventual blocking, not early blocking; timeliness metrics fill this gap.

