10. Contributions and Limitations

10.1 Contributions

1. Unified probabilistic framework: All four layers are defined on a single filtered probability space, eliminating the mixture of deterministic, automata‑theoretic, and Bayesian models that plagued prior work.
2. Exponential consensus bound (Theorem 3.1): A Hoeffding‑based tail bound that provides a computable \epsilon_{\text{cons}}, replacing the qualitative “if‑then” condition of earlier confidence‑weighted consensus analyses.
3. Soundness and CEGAR completeness of the plan verifier (Theorems 4.1–4.2): The first formalisation of LLM‑agent plan verification with an explicit account of the model‑fidelity gap and a finite termination guarantee for abstraction refinement.
4. LTL₃ monitor soundness and liveness impossibility (Theorem 5.1, Lemma 5.2): A rigorous statement of what runtime enforcement can and cannot achieve, with defensible citations.
5. Asymptotic consistency of Bayesian trust (Proposition 5.3): A full Beta‑Bernoulli derivation with an almost‑sure convergence proof, correcting earlier conflations of hypothesis and parameter.
6. Composition theorem with correlation identity (Theorem 7.1, Proposition 7.1): An end‑to‑end bound that uses the Fréchet–Hoeffding inequality as the default guarantee and optionally tightens it using a measurable correlation, without assuming independence.
7. Correct total‑error decomposition (Theorem 7.2): Clarifies that formal verification partitions rather than reduces the total error probability, a governance‑relevant insight.
8. Necessity theorems (Theorems 8.1–8.2): Formal proofs that both the formal verifier and the Bayesian trust layer are indispensable for non‑safety properties and calibration‑free settings.
9. Cost model (§8.3): A concrete complexity analysis that grounds the optimisation objective.

10.2 Limitations

· Correlation \rho must be estimated empirically and may be non‑stationary; the correlation‑aware bound (7.3) is only as tight as the estimate.
· Exchangeability assumption (Proposition 5.3) can fail under distribution shift; non‑stationary environments require adaptive trust mechanisms not yet formalised.
· Sound labeling (Assumption 4.1) remains a manual engineering task; no automated guarantee exists against unmodeled tool side‑effects.
· Truncation monitor restriction: VMAI’s runtime layer cannot enforce liveness, limiting the architecture’s applicability to tasks where plan‑level verification of liveness is sufficient.
· No empirical validation is provided; the evaluation protocol (§9) is a proposal, not a completed study.

