Appendix B. Proofs for Chapter 3 (Consensus Layer)

B.1 Assumptions

Assumption 3.1 (Conditional Independence). Given \mathcal{F}_{t-1}, the pairs (Y_i, p_i) are independent across i = 1,\dots,n.

Assumption 3.2 (Bounded Confidence Gap). There exist constants p_{\min}, p_{\max} \in (0,1) such that almost surely:

· if Z_i = 1 (agent votes correctly), then p_i \ge p_{\min};
· if all incorrect agents vote for the same wrong value y', then for each such agent p_i \le p_{\max}.

B.2 Theorem 3.1 (Exponential Consensus Error Bound)

Let C(y_{\text{true}}) = \sum_{i=1}^n w_i p_i Z_i with weights w_i \mathcal{F}_{t-1}-measurable. Define \mu_h = \mathbb{E}[C(y_{\text{true}})].

(a) For any \epsilon > 0,

\mathbb{P}\bigl( C(y_{\text{true}}) \le \mu_h - \epsilon \bigr)
\le \exp\!\left( -\frac{2\epsilon^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2} \right). \tag{B.1}

(b) If there exists \delta > 0 such that for every incorrect candidate y',

\mu_h - \mathbb{E}[C(y')] \ge \delta, \tag{B.2}

then

\mathbb{P}( y^* \neq y_{\text{true}} ) \le 2 \exp\!\left( -\frac{\delta^2}{2\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2} \right). \tag{B.3}

Proof.
Write C(y_{\text{true}}) = \sum_{i=1}^n X_i with X_i = w_i p_i Z_i. By Assumption 3.1 the X_i are independent conditionally on \mathcal{F}_{t-1} and take values in [0, w_i]. Hoeffding’s inequality (Appendix F) gives

\mathbb{P}\!\left( \sum_{i=1}^n X_i - \mathbb{E}\!\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \le -\epsilon \right)
\le \exp\!\left( -\frac{2\epsilon^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2} \right),

which is (B.1).

For part (b), the error event \{y^* \neq y_{\text{true}}\} implies the existence of an incorrect candidate y' with C(y_{\text{true}}) \le C(y'). By hypothesis (B.2), \mathbb{E}[C(y_{\text{true}})] - \mathbb{E}[C(y')] \ge \delta. Now observe that

\{C(y_{\text{true}}) \le C(y')\} \subseteq
\Bigl\{ C(y_{\text{true}}) \le \mu_h - \tfrac{\delta}{2} \Bigr\} \;\cup\;
\Bigl\{ C(y') \ge \mathbb{E}[C(y')] + \tfrac{\delta}{2} \Bigr\}.

Applying (B.1) to C(y_{\text{true}}) with \epsilon = \delta/2 and a symmetric Hoeffding bound to C(y') (the sum of the independent variables w_i p_i \mathbb{1}[Y_i = y'], also bounded in [0,w_i]) yields

\mathbb{P}(y^* \neq y_{\text{true}}) \le 2 \exp\!\left( -\frac{2(\delta/2)^2}{\sum_i w_i^2} \right)
= 2 \exp\!\left( -\frac{\delta^2}{2\sum_i w_i^2} \right),

completing the proof. ∎

B.3 Corollary 3.1 (Equal Weights)

If w_i = 1/n for all i and calibration is perfect (\gamma_i = 0), then condition (B.2) reduces to (1 - W_f) - W_f \ge \delta, where W_f is the fraction of agents voting incorrectly. Hence an honest majority (W_f < 1/2) suffices to make the error probability exponentially small. This is the crash‑fault honest‑majority bound; the stronger 1/3 Byzantine bound does not apply because the certificate mechanism prevents equivocation.

