Appendix C. Proofs for Chapter 4 (Formal Verification Layer)

C.1 Plan Abstraction

A plan \pi is a finite program in a deterministic, finite‑state plan language. The abstraction function \alpha maps \pi to a finite Kripke structure M_\pi = (S, s_0, \to, L) where:

· S is the set of control locations,
· \to \subseteq S \times S is the transition relation induced by the plan’s control flow,
· L : S \to 2^{\mathrm{AP}} is a sound labelling: if \text{unsafe} \in L(s) then every concrete execution that reaches the state corresponding to s violates the environment’s safety condition.

Specifications are drawn from \Phi_{\text{safe}} = \{ \Box\psi \mid \psi \text{ propositional or past‑LTL} \}.

C.2 Model Checking Procedure

1. Build a Büchi automaton \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} for \neg\varphi = \Diamond\neg\psi. Because \psi is a safety property, \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} is a deterministic finite automaton that accepts exactly the finite bad prefixes (words that contain a violation of \psi).
2. Form the product M_\pi \otimes \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} and check reachability of an accepting state. Set V_{\text{form}}(\varphi,\pi) = 1 if no accepting state is reachable, and 0 otherwise.

C.3 Theorem 4.1 (Soundness)

If V_{\text{form}}(\varphi,\pi) = 1, then every concrete execution trace that respects the control flow of \pi and the sound labelling satisfies \varphi = \Box\psi.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a concrete trace \tau following the plan that violates \Box\psi. Then \tau contains a finite prefix whose last state falsifies \psi; this prefix is a bad prefix and is accepted by \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi}. Because the labelling is sound, the abstract trace of \tau in M_\pi replicates the relevant atomic propositions; thus the product M_\pi \otimes \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} contains an accepting run, contradicting V_{\text{form}} = 1. ∎

C.4 Theorem 4.2 (CEGAR Completeness)

If V_{\text{form}}(\varphi,\pi) = 0, the model checker outputs a finite counterexample trace \sigma in M_\pi. This trace is either real (can be instantiated in a high‑fidelity simulator) or spurious. For spurious counterexamples, a computable refinement procedure adds a new atomic proposition to eliminate that trace. With a finite state space, the refinement loop terminates.

Proof (sketch). The emptiness check yields a path \sigma in M_\pi ending in a state labelled with the violation. The concretisation test simulates \sigma in a faithful environment model. If the simulator can follow \sigma and indeed violates \psi, the counterexample is real. Otherwise, the simulation gets stuck at some step where the abstract transition is not feasible. We refine the labelling by adding a predicate that distinguishes the abstract state from the concrete successors. This removes \sigma from the abstract model. Since the state space S is finite, only finitely many such refinements are possible. ∎

C.5 Complexity (Refinement of Remark 4.1)

When \psi is propositional (no temporal nesting), the Büchi automaton \mathcal{B}_{\neg\varphi} has O(|\psi|) states, and emptiness of the product is decidable in time O(|M_\pi|\cdot |\psi|). If \psi contains nested past operators, the automaton may grow to 2^{O(|\psi|)}, leading to a worst‑case PSPACE bound. The cost model in Chapter 8 uses the linear figure as the typical case.

