Appendix D. Proofs for Chapter 5 (Runtime Verification and Bayesian Trust)

D.1 LTL₃ Monitor (Theorem 5.1)

For \varphi = \Box\psi we construct a three‑valued runtime monitor following Bauer et al. (2011). Given the finite prefix \tau_{0:t}, the monitor returns \bot iff the prefix is a bad prefix (i.e., any infinite continuation violates \varphi), \top iff it is a good prefix (all continuations satisfy \varphi), and ? otherwise. For \Box\psi, a bad prefix is any word that already contains a state where \psi is false. The monitor is realised as a Moore machine that outputs \bot exactly when the next proposed action would transition to a state where \psi would be false.

Theorem 5.1 (Monitor Soundness). If the system blocks the action that triggers the \bot verdict, every finite executed trace satisfies \Box\psi.

Proof. By construction the verdict \bot is raised precisely when the next action would lead to a state falsifying \psi. Blocking that action means the executed trace stops before any such state is entered. Hence the trace contains no position where \psi is false, and therefore satisfies \Box\psi under finite‑trace semantics. ∎

D.2 Lemma 5.2 (Liveness Limitation)

A truncation monitor—whose sole enforcement capability is to halt execution—cannot enforce an arbitrary liveness property \Diamond\,\text{goal}.

Proof (sketch). Schneider (2000) proved that the class of properties enforceable by execution monitors is exactly the set of safety properties. Since \Diamond\,\text{goal} is a liveness property (co‑safety), it is not enforceable by halting alone. An explicit adversary that consistently chooses actions that are safe but avoid the goal will keep the system alive forever, satisfying safety but violating liveness; the monitor cannot force progress because it cannot inject new transitions. ∎

D.3 Bayesian Trust Update (Proposition 5.3)

For each agent, let e_i be the unknown error probability. We place a \text{Beta}(a,b) prior on 1-e_i. After observing K independent tasks, let S_i be the number of correct decisions by agent i. The likelihood is \text{Binomial}(K, 1-e_i). By conjugacy, the posterior of 1-e_i given S_i is \text{Beta}(a+S_i,\; b+K-S_i), with posterior mean

w_i = \mathbb{E}[1-e_i \mid S_i] = \frac{a+S_i}{a+b+K}.

Normalised weights are \tilde{w}_i = w_i / \sum_j w_j.

Proposition 5.3 (Consistency). Under the exchangeability assumption (tasks are i.i.d. for each agent),

\tilde{w}_i \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} \frac{1-e_i}{\sum_{j=1}^n (1-e_j)} \quad \text{as } K \to \infty.

Proof. The Strong Law of Large Numbers implies S_i/K \to 1-e_i almost surely. Write

w_i = \frac{a+b}{a+b+K} \cdot \frac{a}{a+b} + \frac{K}{a+b+K} \cdot \frac{S_i}{K}.

The first term tends to 0 as K\to\infty; hence w_i \to 1-e_i a.s. Normalisation is continuous, so \tilde{w}_i converges to the ratio of limits. ∎

