Appendix E. Proofs for Chapter 7 (Composition Theorem)

Define the events

A = \{\text{consensus accepts an incorrect action}\},\qquad
B = \{\text{formal verifier misses a real violation}\},

with probabilities \epsilon_{\text{cons}} = \mathbb{P}(A), \epsilon_{\text{form}} = \mathbb{P}(B). Let \rho = \mathrm{Corr}(\mathbb{1}_A, \mathbb{1}_B).

Proposition 7.1 (Correlation Identity). For any two events A,B with probabilities in (0,1),

\mathbb{P}(A \cap B) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}}
+ \rho \sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}})\,\epsilon_{\text{form}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{form}})}. \tag{E.1}

Proof. By definition, \mathrm{Cov}(\mathbb{1}_A,\mathbb{1}_B) = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_A\mathbb{1}_B] - \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_A]\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_B] = \mathbb{P}(A\cap B) - \epsilon_{\text{cons}}\epsilon_{\text{form}}. Also, \mathrm{Cov}(\mathbb{1}_A,\mathbb{1}_B) = \rho \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_A)\mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_B)}. For Bernoulli variables, \mathrm{Var}(\mathbb{1}_A) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}}), and similarly for B. Substituting and rearranging gives the identity. ∎

Theorem 7.1 (Composition Bounds). The probability of an unsafe execution satisfies:

(a) Fréchet–Hoeffding (always valid):

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe}) = \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) \le \min(\epsilon_{\text{cons}}, \epsilon_{\text{form}}). \tag{E.2}

(b) Correlation‑aware (if an upper bound \hat{\rho} \ge \rho is available):

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe}) \le \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}}
+ \hat{\rho} \sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{cons}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{cons}})\,\epsilon_{\text{form}}(1-\epsilon_{\text{form}})}. \tag{E.3}

(c) Independence (\rho = 0):

\mathbb{P}(\text{unsafe}) \le \epsilon_{\text{cons}} \epsilon_{\text{form}}. \tag{E.4}

Proof.
(a) Since A \cap B \subseteq A and A \cap B \subseteq B, the monotonicity of \mathbb{P} yields \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) \le \min(\epsilon_{\text{cons}}, \epsilon_{\text{form}}).
(b) From (E.1), \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}}\epsilon_{\text{form}} + \rho \sqrt{\dots}. The square‑root term is non‑negative; replacing \rho by an upper bound \hat{\rho} (which cannot decrease the right‑hand side) gives (E.3).
(c) Set \rho = 0 in (E.1). ∎

Theorem 7.2 (Total Undesired Execution).

\mathbb{P}(\text{undesired execution}) = \epsilon_{\text{cons}},

and the unsafe portion is at most \min(\epsilon_{\text{cons}}, \epsilon_{\text{form}}).

Proof. The event “undesired execution” is exactly A (consensus accepts incorrect), so its probability is \epsilon_{\text{cons}}. Unsafe executions are a subset of A (they require both A and B), so their probability is \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) \le \epsilon_{\text{cons}}. Combining with the Fréchet bound \mathbb{P}(A \cap B) \le \epsilon_{\text{form}} gives the \min bound. ∎

