Appendix F. Proofs for Chapter 8 (Necessity Theorems)

F.1 Theorem 8.1 (Necessity of Formal Verification)

Statement. In a system restricted to consensus and a truncation runtime monitor (no formal verifier), there exist environments where a liveness property \Diamond\,\text{goal} is violated with probability bounded away from zero, irrespective of the number of agents.

Proof. Consider a plan that offers a non‑deterministic choice between two safe actions a_1 and a_2. Only a_1 eventually leads to the goal; a_2 keeps the system in a safe non‑goal region. An adversarial specialist agent consistently proposes a_2. The monitor cannot block a_2 because it is safe. The consensus layer may occasionally select a_1 if other agents propose it, but without a verifier that remembers the plan’s liveness objective, the system cannot force persistent selection of a_1; the adversary can keep offering a_2 indefinitely, causing live‑lock. Hence the probability of eventually reaching the goal can be made arbitrarily small. A formal verifier, by model‑checking \Diamond\,\text{goal} on the abstract plan, would detect the insufficiently constrained plan and force the Planner to produce a plan that deterministically reaches the goal. ∎

F.2 Theorem 8.2 (Necessity of Bayesian Trust)

Statement. If agents are not calibrated (no guarantee on self‑reported p_i), an adversary with minority weight W_f < 1/2 can force an incorrect consensus output with positive probability.

Proof. The adversary’s agents all vote for a wrong value y' and report p_i = 1. Honest agents report their true confidences p_{\text{hon}} < 1. The weighted score for y' is at least W_f \cdot 1. The maximum score for the correct value is (1-W_f)\,p_{\text{hon}}. A sufficient condition for the adversary to win is W_f > (1-W_f)p_{\text{hon}}, i.e. W_f > \frac{p_{\text{hon}}}{1+p_{\text{hon}}}. Because p_{\text{hon}} < 1, the right‑hand side is strictly less than 1/2; thus there exists a minority weight W_f < 1/2 that satisfies this inequality. Without calibration, the consensus layer cannot distinguish true confidence from bluffed confidence. The Bayesian trust layer, by accumulating historical accuracy data, would eventually down‑weight agents who consistently report high confidence but perform poorly, thereby neutralising the bluff. ∎

